(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-introduction-to-cam-quiz"]={"title":"Introduction to CAM — CAM/Nutrition Exam 2","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Gua sha is also known by which other names, and what is it used for?","choices":["Coining or spooning, used mainly for anxiety and insomnia in adult patients","Scraping, coining, or spooning, used commonly for neck and back musculoskeletal pain","Cupping or suctioning, used commonly for improving balance and physical function","Tapping or percussing, used mainly for nausea and procedure-related distress in adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives scraping, coining, and spooning as alternate names, describes repeated strokes with a smooth-edged instrument, and lists musculoskeletal pain of the neck and back as common uses. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How does the lecture define conventional healthcare?","choices":["A system whose practitioners treat symptoms and disease using drugs, radiation, or surgery","A system whose practitioners treat the whole person using diet, herbs, and manual therapy","A system whose practitioners restore balance among the body's three fundamental energies","A system whose practitioners direct healing energy through the hands toward the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines conventional healthcare as a system in which professionals such as physicians, physician assistants, nurse practitioners, pharmacists, physical therapists, and nurses treat symptoms and diseases using drugs, radiation, or surgery. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which statement reflects the lecture's definition of hypnosis?","choices":["A sleep state during which the patient loses awareness of the surroundings","A state of focused attention and increased responsiveness to therapeutic suggestion","A condition in which the therapist assumes control of the patient's decisions","A form of entertainment without any recognized clinical application"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines hypnosis as a state of focused attention with reduced peripheral awareness and an enhanced capacity for response to suggestion. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which set of terms does the lecture list as alternate names for conventional healthcare?","choices":["Integrative medicine, functional medicine, and naturopathic medicine","Ayurveda, homeopathy, naturopathy, and traditional Chinese medicine","Allopathic, biomedicine, mainstream, orthodox, and Western medicine","Biofield therapy, energy medicine, and manipulative body-based practice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these as other names for conventional healthcare, the system in which professionals treat symptoms and disease using drugs, radiation, or surgery. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of action of chelating drugs as the lecture describes it?","choices":["They stimulate the kidney to increase overall urine production","They neutralize metals chemically so the metals become inert within tissue","They bind with metals and enhance their excretion from the body","They block absorption of metals across the intestinal wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this mechanism, with established uses in lead poisoning, iron overload, and other heavy metal toxicities. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is Qi and how is it believed to travel?","choices":["A dosha determined at birth that travels through the bloodstream","A vital force of life that flows along pathways called meridians","A biofield generated by the practitioner and transferred through the hands","An electrical signal measured by sensors placed on the skin surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes Qi, pronounced \"chee,\" as a vital force of life that travels along pathways called meridians, sometimes described as an energy highway. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which set of practices does the lecture give as common within traditional Chinese medicine?","choices":["Hydrotherapy, exercise therapy, nutritional supplementation, and mind-body therapy","Chelation infusion, botanical extraction, specialized diets, and supplement regimens","Progressive relaxation, guided imagery, hypnotherapy, and mantra repetition","Acupuncture, herbal medicine, dietary therapy, massage, tai chi, and qigong"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as the common practices of traditional Chinese medicine, which originated in China and evolved over thousands of years. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which physiologic functions does the lecture list as monitorable during biofeedback?","choices":["Serum electrolyte concentration, blood glucose, and circulating hormone levels","Heart rate, blood pressure, muscle activity, skin temperature, breathing, and brain activity","Bone mineral density, joint range of motion, and overall muscular strength","Visual acuity, auditory threshold, and peripheral sensation to light touch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these physiologic functions as measurable by the electronic monitoring devices used in biofeedback. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"From which language and meaning is the term Ayurveda derived, according to the lecture?","choices":["Greek, from words meaning \"like\" and \"disease\"","Chinese, from separate words meaning \"vital force\" and \"energy pathway\"","Latin, from words meaning \"nature\" and \"healing power\"","Sanskrit, from ayur meaning life and veda meaning knowledge or science"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that Ayurveda comes from the Sanskrit words ayur, meaning life, and veda, meaning knowledge or science. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The lecture credits Hippocrates with which of the following?","choices":["Founding the first naturopathic medical college in North America","Being called the Father of Medicine and saying \"Let food be thy medicine\"","Developing the law of similars that underlies homeopathic prescribing","Establishing the meridian system through which the vital force flows"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls Hippocrates one of the most outstanding figures in the history of medicine, often referred to as the Father of Medicine, and credits him with the saying \"Let food be thy medicine.\" — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does the lecture define botanical medicine?","choices":["The use of plants or plant extracts for medicinal purposes, including algae and macroscopic fungi","The use of chelating agents that bind metals and enhance their excretion","The use of specialized therapeutic dietary patterns to prevent and to manage chronic disease","The use of vitamins and minerals to correct measured laboratory deficiencies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines botanical medicine as the use of plants or plant extracts medicinally, consisting of plant materials, algae, macroscopic fungi, or combinations, available as solutions, powders, tablets, capsules, elixirs, topicals, or injections. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the lecture say modern research suggests about how acupuncture may work?","choices":["It corrects measurable deficiencies of trace minerals in the bloodstream","It produces its effects entirely through patient expectation with no physiologic correlate","It works by diluting and shaking the active substance before administration","It may influence pain-processing pathways and benefit selected pain conditions and symptom management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes that in traditional Chinese medicine acupuncture is believed to influence the flow of Qi along meridians, while modern research suggests it may influence pain-processing pathways. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What belief underlies reflexology as the lecture describes it?","choices":["That spinal alignment determines the function of every peripheral joint","That specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears correspond to specific organs or body systems","That repeated dilution of a remedy increases its therapeutic potency","That the balance among three fundamental body energies is what determines overall health"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that reflexology involves applying pressure to specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears, which practitioners believe correspond to particular organs or body systems. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which statement about hypnosis does the lecture identify as a myth?","choices":["Individuals differ in how responsive they are to hypnotic suggestion","Clinical hypnotherapy uses guided relaxation and therapeutic suggestion","People lose consciousness or memory of what happened during the session","The precise mechanism underlying hypnosis remains poorly understood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists this among the myths, clarifying that most individuals remain aware and remember the session afterward. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient continues her oncologist's chemotherapy regimen and also attends weekly acupuncture sessions for nausea. Using the lecture's terminology, the acupuncture is best described as which of the following?","choices":["Complementary medicine, because it is used together with conventional medicine","Alternative medicine, because it is used in place of conventional medicine","Conventional medicine, because a licensed clinician recommended it","Allopathic medicine, because it is delivered inside a hospital system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines complementary medicine as an approach used TOGETHER with conventional medicine, which is exactly what adding acupuncture to an ongoing chemotherapy regimen represents. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where does the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health sit within the federal government, according to the lecture?","choices":["It is an independent agency reporting directly to the office of the Surgeon General","It is a division of the Food and Drug Administration responsible for supplement approval","It is a branch of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, operating within the United States Public Health Service","It is part of the National Institutes of Health within the United States Department of Health and Human Services"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture identifies it as the federal government's lead agency for scientific research on complementary and integrative health, sitting within the National Institutes of Health under the Department of Health and Human Services. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient describes a clinic offering an infusion that \"cures cancer,\" available only at a facility abroad. Based on the lecture's counseling questions, which feature is the strongest warning sign?","choices":["The practitioner makes unfounded claims such as the ability to cure cancer","The therapy is delivered by infusion rather than by mouth","The therapy has been studied in more than one clinical trial","The treatment is being offered through an established academic medical institution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists unfounded claims such as curing cancer among the specific questions clinicians should raise with patients, alongside travel requirements and cost. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What physiologic changes does the lecture associate with the relaxation response?","choices":["Faster breathing, increased heart rate, and higher blood pressure","Slower breathing, decreased heart rate, and lower blood pressure","Unchanged vital signs with improvement only in reported mood","Rising body temperature with a fall in measured muscle tension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that relaxation therapy promotes the relaxation response, which counteracts the physiologic effects of stress and is associated with slower breathing, decreased heart rate, and lower blood pressure. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which reason for the popularity of complementary and alternative medicine is listed in the lecture?","choices":["Insurance plans reimburse these therapies at higher rates than conventional care","Federal regulation guarantees the purity of every product sold","A desire for greater control over one's personal health","Clinicians receive extensive formal training in these therapies during medical education"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists a desire for greater control over personal health among the reasons for popularity, alongside the perception that these approaches are natural and safe. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which treatment methods does the lecture list as part of an Ayurvedic treatment plan?","choices":["Diet, herbs, massage, meditation, yoga, and internal cleansing","Needles, cupping vessels, scraping tools, and spinal adjustment","Diluted remedies, succussion, and matched symptom prescribing","Sensors, monitors, real-time displays, and guided practice sessions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that after determining the balance of doshas, practitioners design an individualized plan using diet, herbs, massage, meditation, yoga, and internal cleansing, also called therapeutic elimination. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the lecture conclude about the evidence supporting cupping?","choices":["Evidence is strong and supports cupping as a first-line treatment for back pain","Evidence shows benefit only when cupping is combined with spinal manipulation","Evidence is limited, and the benefits that are seen appear to be primarily short term","Evidence demonstrates no measurable effect on local blood flow in any study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that evidence for cupping is limited and that benefits appear to be primarily short term, while noting it is commonly used for neck and low back pain. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A clinician teaches a patient to systematically tense and then release muscle groups throughout the body. Which technique is this?","choices":["Progressive muscle relaxation, which helps patients recognize and reduce muscle tension","Guided imagery, which uses mental images and several senses to build a calming scene","Electromyographic biofeedback, which converts muscle signals into a visible display","Transcendental meditation, which uses silent repetition of a mantra during practice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists progressive muscle relaxation as a relaxation technique in which patients systematically tense and relax muscle groups, helping them recognize and reduce tension. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"The lecture's clinical pearl warns that many patients do not disclose their use of complementary approaches. Which explanation is given?","choices":["Patients are legally prohibited from discussing any unregulated product with a licensed clinician during a documented visit","Patients assume electronic health records automatically capture supplement purchases","Patients are usually asked about these therapies and choose to decline answering","Some believe providers will disapprove, and others do not consider supplements or herbal products to be medications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives both reasons and instructs clinicians to ask specifically about complementary and alternative medicine use in a nonjudgmental manner. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A homeopathic remedy called Allium cepa, made from onion, is offered for a patient with a runny nose and watery eyes. Which homeopathic theory does this illustrate?","choices":["The law of minimum dose, because the onion is diluted before use","The doctrine of biofield transfer, in which healing energy passes through the practitioner's hands","The principle that health depends on balance between yin and yang","The law of similars, because a substance that causes symptoms in healthy people is used to treat those same symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture uses this exact example: cutting onions causes burning, teary eyes and a runny nose, so Allium cepa is used for colds or hay fever under the \"like cures like\" principle. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which description matches transcendental meditation as the lecture presents it?","choices":["Systematically tensing and relaxing muscle groups throughout the body","Being intensely aware of what one senses and feels in the moment without interpretation or judgment","Silently repeating a mantra, traditionally practiced for twenty minutes twice daily","Using electronic monitoring to display physiologic function in real time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes transcendental meditation as silently repeating a mantra, traditionally for twenty minutes twice daily, studied for stress, anxiety, and cardiovascular health. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does the lecture report about evidence for the clinical benefits of Reiki?","choices":["Evidence for specific clinical benefits remains limited and inconclusive","Evidence confirms measurable transfer of energy between practitioner and patient","Evidence supports Reiki as an established first-line therapy for chronic pain","Evidence shows benefit only when the practitioner touches the skin directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that Reiki is commonly used for relaxation, stress reduction, and overall well-being, but that evidence for specific clinical benefits remains limited and inconclusive. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which action does the lecture recommend as part of the clinician's role regarding complementary and alternative medicine?","choices":["Discourage all use until a therapy has received federal approval","Refer all questions about these therapies to a naturopathic doctor","Monitor for safety concerns, including herb-drug and supplement-drug interactions","Document a patient's use only in those cases where the patient raises the subject independently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture explicitly lists monitoring for safety concerns including herb-drug and supplement-drug interactions among the clinician's responsibilities. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which condition does the lecture list among the indications for hypnotherapy?","choices":["Irritable bowel syndrome","Iron overload requiring metal binding","Lead poisoning from environmental exposure","Pelvic floor dysfunction managed by muscle sensors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists irritable bowel syndrome among hypnotherapy indications, alongside acute and chronic pain, anxiety, phobias, nausea and vomiting, procedure-related distress, habit disorders, insomnia, and childbirth support. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which body systems do manipulative and body-based practices target, according to the lecture?","choices":["The endocrine glands together with the hormones they release into the circulation","The proposed biofields believed to surround the body","The central and peripheral nervous system pathways","The musculoskeletal system and soft tissues, including bones, joints, muscles, and connective tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines this domain by its focus on the musculoskeletal system and soft tissues, using hands-on techniques to improve function, reduce pain, and promote well-being. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What does the law of minimum dose hold, and what process accompanies it?","choices":["The lower the dose, the greater the therapeutic effect, achieved through repeated dilution and shaking called succussion","The dose must be calculated by body weight, with adjustment through titration","The smallest clinically effective dose should first be identified and then maintained by continuous steady infusion","The dose should be doubled at each visit until symptoms resolve completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the law of minimum dose as the belief that lower doses produce greater therapeutic effect, and notes that many remedies undergo repeated dilution and shaking, a process called succussion. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
